    <item>
      <title>Slackware-Based Zenwalk ISOs Updated With Linux 7.1, Flatpak Integrated + Xfce 4.20</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Zenwalk-2026-Current</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Zenwalk-2026-Current</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 22:27:47 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; It's been over four years since the original release of Zenwalk 15.0 while released today is a new Zenwalk Current Milestone snapshot. This Slackware-based Linux distribution with a long history has an exciting update out for testing this weekend. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Following the February 2022 release of &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Slackware-15.0-Released"&gt;Slackware 15.0&lt;/a&gt; was the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Zenwalk-15"&gt;Zenwalk 15 release&lt;/a&gt; for that Linux distribution that's also been around for many years. Here's what Zenwalk looked like twenty years ago when covered on Phoronix: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=505&amp;image=zenlive_26_05_med" alt="Zenwalk in 2006" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Or Phoronix at the time in 2006 on Zenwalk: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=505&amp;image=zenlive_26_04_med" alt=-"Zenwalk" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Lots of fond memories with Zenwalk (and Slackware), so fun to see today the new current milestone release for an updated experience of this distribution. The Zenwalk Current Milestone ships the latest Slackware upstream paired with Zenwalk's own system improvements and desktop enhancements. It was just last month that they were pursuing some &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Zenwalk-BORE-Kernel"&gt;low-latency desktop enhancements&lt;/a&gt; on Zenwalk with the BORE scheduler. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Zenwalk Current Milestone completely integrates Flatpak package support into the distribution, the Linux 7.1 kernel is used by default with the new BORE scheduler, and Xfce 4.20 provides the default desktop base. There is also a new graphical system toolbox with Zenwalk, improved media support, and various other changes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;And for what Zenwalk is looking like in 2026: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=zenwalk_2026" alt="Zenwalk Current Milestone" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on the Zenwalk Current Milestone 2026 release via &lt;a href="https://www.zenwalk.org/2026/09/zenwalk-current-milestone-2026.html"&gt;Zenwalk.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ikey Doherty Plans To Show How To "Properly" Build A Modern Linux Distro</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ikey-Doherty-Plans-Linux-Build</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ikey-Doherty-Plans-Linux-Build</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 18:35:41 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Ikey+Doherty"&gt;Ikey Doherty&lt;/a&gt; is back in the Linux distribution space. Sort of. Ikey Doherty who was the founder of the Solus Linux distribution as well as Serpent OS (now AerynOS) as well as having been employed by Intel on their Clear Linux team is back at it again in the Linux space.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;While being a full-time traveler and content creator, Ikey Doherty wrote into Phoronix to share his upcoming Linux plans. He says he is "not making another distro" or not directly, but does intend to publicly showcase how he feels a modern Linux distribution should be built. That will also include building his own new public package manager.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;He explained that he has "declared war on the industry" and with this latest Linux distribution effort not planning on maintaining the distribution long-term but planning to just publicly highlight how it should be done. He envisions it as sort of like "Clear Linux meets Serpent".  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=barney" alt="Barney Linux distro" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;For those wanting to follow along on his journey for publicly chronicling how he feels modern Linux distributions should be built, he'll be publishing updates via his &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/ikeydoherty/recent-activity/all/"&gt;LinkedIn&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/@ikey.doherty"&gt;YouTube&lt;/a&gt;.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/3eq_zXS5Bhw?si=gsbUi-IZKXY91Sfh" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;In his initial video made public today, Ikey outlined the plans as a source-based, fully-optimized, update-friendly and immutable Linux distribution. And leveraging the Rust programming language as much as possible. It's being developed under the &lt;a href="https://github.com/TravellingEngineer/barney"&gt;Barney&lt;/a&gt; name.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>NVIDIA Posts vGPU Manager &amp; VFIO Variant Driver For Open-Source Nova</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/NVIDIA-Nova-vGPU-Manager</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/NVIDIA-Nova-vGPU-Manager</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 15:02:15 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="NVIDIA" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/nvidia.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; NVIDIA continues building out more functionality around the open-source, upstream Nova kernel graphics driver within the Linux kernel. Making it out to the mailing list this Saturday is a set of 13 patches for introducing a NVIDIA vGPU manager and VFIO variant driver.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The patches are for enabling vGPU management and supporting VFIO users with QEMU for either Linux or Windows guests, all while running off the upstream Nova kernel driver. NVIDIA continues investing quite a lot into the Nova driver stack and back when I was at NVIDIA HQ earlier this year there were some very promising remarks made about the future of NVIDIA on Linux with Nova.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The diagram shared for showing the control path enabled by this NVIDIA vGPU manager and VFIO variant driver:  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=nvidia_vgpu_manager" alt="NVIDIA diagram" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Before getting too excited by some readers whenever seeing "VFIO" mentioned around GPUs, the NVIDIA physical GPU is more than likely just their workstation and data center products to be supported and not the consumer GeForce GPUs.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Those curious about this NVIDIA vGPU manager and VFIO code can learn more via &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260905081116.106613-1-zhiw@nvidia.com/"&gt;this patch series&lt;/a&gt;. The NVIDIA Nova driver continues marching along and will be interesting to when it's ultimately ready for end-user use on the mainline kernel.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Nouveau Lands Display Fixes For NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs As It Works Toward HDMI 2.1</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Nouveau-Blackwell-Display-Fixes</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Nouveau-Blackwell-Display-Fixes</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 11:20:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="NOUVEAU" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/nouveau.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; This week's Direct Rendering Manager (DRM) fixes that were merged ahead of Sunday's Linux 7.3-rc2 release contain a number of fixes to the open-source Nouveau driver's display support for current-generation NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There are a number of display-related fixes merged for the Nouveau kernel driver for better interactions with the GPU System Processor (GSP), fixing HDMI vendor infoframes for GB20x hardware, fixing HDMI GCP AVMute register offsets, fixing vblank interrupts on Blackwell GPUs, and other fixes. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=nvidia-geforce-rtx-5090-pre&amp;image=nvidia_rtx5090_7_med" alt="NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090 display connectors" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Nouveau developer Mohamed Ahmed mentioned in &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260820164929.17117-1-mohamedahmedegypt2001@gmail.com/"&gt;the patch series&lt;/a&gt; of these Nouveau Blackwell display fixes that they largely came about while working toward Nouveau's HDMI 2.1 support: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Several fixes for GSP-driven displays consisting of four independent groups. These were created as part of ongoing HDMI 2.1 bring up and display handling clean-up work, so some of them are not issues now (e.g., the 2.147GHz pixel clock cap) but fixing them is required to get advanced features such as FRL, DSC, VRR, etc working properly."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;These fixes were merged overnight as part of the &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/CAPM=9ty9kjb1bKFgjgBS1tgM3zP2tK+5qTFzT9-0t+1jykBDxw@mail.gmail.com/"&gt;DRM fixes&lt;/a&gt; for Linux 7.3-rc2. There are also a number of AMDGPU and Intel driver fixes too, among fixes to the smaller DRM drivers as well.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux Patches Posted For Initial Enablement Of The Apple A18 Pro + MacBook Neo</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Apple-A18-Pro-MacBook-Neo-Patch</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Apple-A18-Pro-MacBook-Neo-Patch</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 11:11:11 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="APPLE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/apple.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; In addition to Linux developers focused on Apple  Silicon being busy enabling functionality for the M3 and newer SoCs under the Linux kernel, there are also developers working on enabling the A18 Pro SoC used by the new, budget-friendly MacBook Neo laptop. Patches were posted this week for initial enablement of the A18 Pro and the MacBook Neo Device Tree... But even with these patches, the Linux support is still severely limited for now. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Open-source developer Yureka Lilian posted the initial Linux patches on Friday for laying out the Device Trees and bindings for the Apple A18 Pro SoC and the recently-launched MacBook Neo low-cost laptop. But similar to the current Apple M3 Linux support at the moment, it's severely limited. In fact, all the more limited right now for the A18 Pro with currently just being able to successfully run on a single CPU core. The current single core restriction is due to WFI and WFIT instructions. This is just the start of the long process for getting the MacBook Neo to ultimately run nice on Linux. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=macbook_neo_1_med" alt="Apple MacBook Neo" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Beyond the Linux kernel changes for being able to boot on the MacBook Neo, there are also changes needed to the Asahi Linux project's m1n1 bootloader. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Added a device tree for the MacBook Neo, which uses the t8140 SoC (A18 Pro) also found in the iPhone 16 Pro. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The device tree has a minimal set of devices limited to CPU cores, interrupt controller, watchdog, serial and framebuffer."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;It will likely be quite some months before the MacBook Neo is working nicely on Linux with GPU acceleration and all the functionality expected out of a modern laptop for daily use, but this is the first step being achieved now. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=macbook_neo_2_med" alt="Apple MacBook Neo top view" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The initial patches are out for review on the &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260904-apple-a18-pro-initial-devicetrees-v1-0-0c25401d165d@cyberchaos.dev/"&gt;Linux kernel mailing list&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMD Working To "Push Rust Deep Into The GPU Stack"</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Rust-Deep-Into-GPU-Stack</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Rust-Deep-Into-GPU-Stack</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 10:48:48 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; AMD is building out what they are describing as an "elite" team of developers for driving Rust code "deep into the GPU stack" from firmware to drivers, shader compilers, and other GPU software in Rust. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;AMD Senior Fellow Harsh Meno posted this week to &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7498974645694586880/"&gt;LinkedIn&lt;/a&gt; about this new effort for driving Rust into the AMD GPU software stack: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"We’re building a small, elite team at AMD to push Rust deep into the GPU stack. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Compilers. Runtimes. Firmware. GPU architecture. Hardware/software co-design."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;And noted &lt;a href="https://careers.amd.com/careers-home/jobs/89874?lang=en-us"&gt;this AMD job posting&lt;/a&gt; that's active for hiriing a software development engineer to work on Rust code, The role description is quite interesting: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"We are building next-generation systems software for AMD GPUs with Rust as a core technical direction. The work spans compilers, runtimes, low-level GPU software, firmware, developer tooling, and methods for improving the safety and correctness of complex hardware-software systems. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Rust is not incidental to this role. You will help establish how Rust is used in performance-sensitive and high-trust parts of the GPU stack, including the compiler and tooling support, system interfaces, engineering practices, and validation methods required for production deployment. You will have the opportunity to influence both near-term implementations and the longer-term architecture for using Rust across current and future AMD platforms."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;It will be very interesting to see what  comes of this new Rust initiative within AMD. AMD also isn't alone as NVIDIA has also been pursuing similar Rust-based initiatives for their GPU software too, including the development of the Nova Linux kernel driver written in Rust.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GNOME Stakeholders Discussing RFC Process For Solving Major Technical Changes</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-RFC-Process-Discussion</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-RFC-Process-Discussion</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 10:31:45 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; GNOME has been &lt;a hre=f"https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-RFC-Process-AI-Extensions" href="https://www.phoronix.com/"&gt;working to establish a formal request for comments (RFC) process&lt;/a&gt; for solving major technical questions and changes moving forward. A drafted RFC is now under discussion by GNOME stakeholders for moving this process forward. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Sophie Herold published the draft RFC process this week for discussion. This is to ultimately succeed the long-used, ad-hoc and informal process by GNOME developers in making major changes. The proposed RFCC comes down to: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;## Summary &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;* RFCs will be used as the process for proposing major changes which have broad repercussions &lt;br /&gt;* Each RFC will be a written proposal which is added to the RFC project as a merge request &lt;br /&gt;* Stakeholders will be identified and notified of each RFC; teams can also identify themselves as stakeholders &lt;br /&gt;* The discussion of an RFC happens on Discourse &lt;br /&gt;* Anyone can raise concerns about an RFC, work to resolve these is made after the concern has been accepted by the RFC editors &lt;br /&gt;* Anyone can propose changes to an RFC &lt;br /&gt;* Once discussion has died down, the final comment period will be announced, with a proposal to either accept or reject the RFC. After this point, there are 14 days to raise new concerns before the RFC is accepted/rejected.&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those curious about this proposal can find it in full via &lt;a href="https://gitlab.gnome.org/sophie-h/rfcs/-/merge_requests/1"&gt;this merge request&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The RFC is being discussed on &lt;a href="https://discourse.gnome.org/t/proposal-for-an-rfc-process/37221"&gt;GNOME Discourse&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_matrix" alt="GNOME Matrix Shell extension demo" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In addition to the RFC discussion kicking off this week, &lt;a href="https://thisweek.gnome.org/posts/2026/09/twig-264/"&gt;This Week in GNOME&lt;/a&gt; also highlighted a number of other recent GNOME changes. Some of the other activities include enhancements made to the Nautilus file manager this cycle and even a new GNOME Shell extension for The Matrix-inspired falling digital rain on your desktop.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Plasma 6.8 Lands Windows-Like Auto-Scrolling, Lower vRAM Usage For Breeze Theme</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Autoscroll</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Autoscroll</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 21:00:04 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; It's been an exciting start to the month of September for KDE Plasma developers with continuing to land last minute improvements for Plasma 6.8 ahead of its official release in mid-October. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In addition to KDE Plasma 6.8 this week seeing &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Dwell-Clicker"&gt;dwell clicker support to address an accessibility feature gap on Wayland&lt;/a&gt; compared to KMouseTool found in the KDE Plasma X11 session, another exciting feature also landed... Windows-like Autoscroll! KDE Plasma 6.8 will support an Autoscroll feature where you can press the middle mouse button to enter a dedicated scrolling mode and using the pointer to scroll. KDE Plasma did support the feature when you pressed and held the middle mouse button, but now there's an option to toggle the mode by pressing the middle mouse button. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 also added a new remote desktop mode "enable exclusive mode" that locks the remote computer's physical screens upon connection while showing a newly-created virtual screen to the user who is connecting. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;And another exciting one for the week... Union styles will now show in the KDE System Settings' Application Styles page. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 will also feature higher quality screen recordings made by Spectacle and other KPipeWire-using apps. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For those with limited vRAM capacities, Plasma 6.8's Breeze theme will now use less vRAM thanks to optimizing some of the SVG assets.  &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the many exciting KDE Plasma 6.8 changes to kick off the month of September can be found via &lt;a href="https://blogs.kde.org/2026/09/04/this-week-in-plasma-auto-scrolling-and-dwell-clicking/"&gt;This Week in Plasma&lt;/a&gt;. KDE Plasma 6.8 is working its way toward its stable debut on 14 October that will also mark the 30th anniversary of the KDE project.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Wine 11.17 Released With Initial Support For Display Mode Emulation</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.17-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.17-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 20:11:12 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="WINE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/wine.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Wine 11.17 is out today as the newest bi-weekly feature development release for this software enabling Windows games and apps to run on Linux. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Arguably most exciting with today's Wine 11.17 release is having initial display mode emulation support. Rémi Bernon led this work to allow OpenGL scaling according to the emulated resolution. It's also described as OpenGL rendering virtualization. The rendering is done to the expected virtual size and then rendered to the actual physical-sized host window. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.17 also pulls in the new &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/VKD3D-2.1-Released"&gt;VKD3D 2.1&lt;/a&gt; release for Direct3D 12 atop Vulkan.  &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine  11.17 additionally has better support for preferred UI languages and then a total of 44 known bug fixes. The bug fixes for the past two weeks really are wide-ranging from different new and old games to many app fixes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The full list of Wine 11.17 changes can be found via the release announcement on &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/releases/wine-11.17"&gt;WineHQ.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Amazon Linux 2027 Shows Off Nice Performance Gains On AMD EPYC In The AWS Cloud</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/review/amazon-linux-2027-benchmarks</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/review/amazon-linux-2027-benchmarks</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 15:33:21 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Yesterday &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Amazon-Linux-2027-Preview"&gt;AWS announced the public preview of Amazon Linux 2027&lt;/a&gt; as their successor to the current Amazon Linux 2023 in-house distribution. With all the software upgrades and improvements like building packages now with link-time optimizations (LTO), enforcing x86-64-v3 builds, and other changes, I was eager to run some performance benchmarks of Amazon Linux 2023 vs. Amazon Linux 2027 in the EC2 cloud.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=amazon-linux-2027-benchmarks&amp;image=amazon_linux_2027_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=amazon-linux-2027-benchmarks&amp;image=amazon_linux_2027_1_med" alt="Amazon Linux 2027" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Amazon Linux 2027 comes with many big package upgrades like Linux 7.1 as its default kernel compared to Linux 6.18 that is the current default on Amazon Linux 2023. The compiler leap is a big one going from GCC 11.5 by default to the new GCC 16.1. There are also other key upgrades like moving from Python 3.9 to Python 3.14. Some package updates aren't so significant like OpenJDK 25~26 with already having brought those updates back to Amazon Linux 2023.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=amazon-linux-2027-benchmarks&amp;image=amazon_linux_2027_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=amazon-linux-2027-benchmarks&amp;image=amazon_linux_2027_2_med" alt="Amazon Linux 2027" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;But beyond the package version upgrades, with Amazon Linux 2027 now using LTO compiler optimizations, x86-64-v3 to assume AVX/AVX2, and other fundamental improvements, it makes for an interesting benchmarking session. Amazon Linux 2027 packages are roughly based on Fedora 44~45 sources plus changes.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="https://phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/result.svgz" alt="Amazon Linux 2027 Performance Benchmarks" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Since yesterday I have been running fresh benchmarks of Amazon Linux 2023 in its current state against the new public preview of Amazon Linux 2027. Due to not being comped any cloud credits and the persistently very tight operating budget for Phoronix, I limited the Amazon Linux benchmarking to a single instance using the AMD EPYC 5th Gen "Turin" using the general purpose m8a.4xlarge instance, given the popularity of cloud VMs at the 16 vCPU size being the most popular. The number of benchmarks run was also rather limited compared to my typical testing, again to minimize costs amid these difficult times in the web publishing space.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The EPYC 9R45 with 16 vCPUs was paired with 64GB of DDR5-6400 memory and 400GB of local storage. From there  Amazon Linux 2023 and Amazon Linux 2027 m8a.4xlarge benchmarks were conducted to see what the out-of-the-box performance means for this new Amazon Linux release.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/openradioss-cpdt.svgz" alt="OpenRadioss benchmark with settings of Model: Cell Phone Drop Test. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/openradioss-rorsi.svgz" alt="OpenRadioss benchmark with settings of Model: Rubber O-Ring Seal Installation. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Of the HPC workloads tested, Amazon Linux 2027 in its preview form was typically running slightly faster than Amazon Linux 2023. AMD EPYC Turin already is well supported and running great on Amazon Linux 2023 while now is just some icing on the cake for many of these benchmarks.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dacapo-benchmark-eclipse.svgz" alt="DaCapo Benchmark benchmark with settings of Java Test: Eclipse. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dacapo-benchmark-apache-tomcat.svgz" alt="DaCapo Benchmark benchmark with settings of Java Test: Apache Tomcat. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dacapo-benchmark-psca.svgz" alt="DaCapo Benchmark benchmark with settings of Java Test: PMD Source Code Analyzer. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Due to Amazon Linux 2023 updating its default OpenJDK Java version, there wasn't much to see out of the Java workloads tested.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/zstd-compression-19-compression-speed.svgz" alt="Zstd Compression benchmark with settings of Compression Level: 19, Compression Speed. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The OS-supplied Zstd package was slightly faster on Amazon Linux 2027 in moving from Zstandard 1.5.5 to t.5.7 plus the package build optimizations.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/7-zip-compression-compression-rating.svgz" alt="7-Zip Compression benchmark with settings of Test: Compression Rating. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When compiling 7-Zip from source, the default toolchain with Amazon Linux 2027 was providing faster performance than when built and run under Amazon Linux 2023.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/7-zip-compression-compression-rating-1.svgz" alt="7-Zip Compression benchmark with settings of Test: Compression Rating. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When testing the operating system supplied 7-Zip binaries, Amazon Linux 2027 was also faster than AL2023 and there it moved from 7-Zip 25.01 to 26.02.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/timed-linux-kernel-compilation-defconfig.svgz" alt="Timed Linux Kernel Compilation benchmark with settings of Build: defconfig. Amazon Linux 2023.12.20260831 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/timed-linux-kernel-compilation-allmodconfig.svgz" alt="Timed Linux Kernel Compilation benchmark with settings of Build: allmodconfig. Amazon Linux 2023.12.20260831 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When measuring the time to compile different programs, Amazon Linux 2027 was slightly slower than AL2023. But not unexpected. Typically the newer compilers add new optimizations and other features at the expense of slightly longer build times. From AL2023 to AL2027 is moving from GCC 11 to GCC 16.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/openssl-sha256.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: SHA256. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/openssl-chacha20.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: ChaCha20. Amazon Linux 2023.12.20260831 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/openssl-aes-256-gcm.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: AES-256-GCM. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;OpenSSL 3.5 was supplied on both Amazon Linux distributions while the AL2027 build came out slightly faster than AL2023.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dragonflydb-10-15.svgz" alt="Dragonflydb benchmark with settings of Clients Per Thread: 10, Set To Get Ratio: 1:5. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dragonflydb-20-15.svgz" alt="Dragonflydb benchmark with settings of Clients Per Thread: 20, Set To Get Ratio: 1:5. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dragonflydb-10-110.svgz" alt="Dragonflydb benchmark with settings of Clients Per Thread: 10, Set To Get Ratio: 1:10. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dragonflydb-20-110.svgz" alt="Dragonflydb benchmark with settings of Clients Per Thread: 20, Set To Get Ratio: 1:10. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dragonflydb-10-1100.svgz" alt="Dragonflydb benchmark with settings of Clients Per Thread: 10, Set To Get Ratio: 1:100. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/dragonflydb-20-1100.svgz" alt="Dragonflydb benchmark with settings of Clients Per Thread: 20, Set To Get Ratio: 1:100. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Dragonflydb database built from source was showing off some nice performance wins on Amazon Linux 2023 with this AMD EPYC VM.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/cryptsetup-pbkdf2-sha512.svgz" alt="Cryptsetup benchmark with settings of PBKDF2-sha512. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/cryptsetup-ax2e.svgz" alt="Cryptsetup benchmark with settings of AES-XTS 256b Encryption. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/cryptsetup-ax2d.svgz" alt="Cryptsetup benchmark with settings of AES-XTS 256b Decryption. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/cryptsetup-ax5e.svgz" alt="Cryptsetup benchmark with settings of AES-XTS 512b Encryption. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/cryptsetup-ax5d.svgz" alt="Cryptsetup benchmark with settings of AES-XTS 512b Decryption. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Cryptsetup for exercising the Linux kernel's crypto code was showing some nice performance gains under Amazon Linux 2027.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/pybench-tfatt.svgz" alt="PyBench benchmark with settings of Total For Average Test Times. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With the move from Python 3.9 to Python 3.14, Amazon Linux 2027 delivered some nice performance gains for Python on this AMD EPYC M8A instance.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/llamacpp-cpu-blas-llama-31-tulu-3-8b-q8_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: Llama-3.1-Tulu-3-8B-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Amazon Linux 2023.12.20260831 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/llamacpp-cpu-blas-mistral-7b-instruct-v03-q8_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Amazon Linux 2023.12.20260831 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Llama.cpp AI CPU-based performance was largely the same between these Amazon Linux tests but again the scope of the testing was more limited than normal to minimize cloud costs.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amazon-linux-2027-performance-benchmarks/geometric-mean-of-all-test-results-result-composite-al2pb.svgz" alt="Geometric Mean Of All Test Results benchmark with settings of Result Composite, Amazon Linux 2027 Performance Benchmarks. Amazon Linux 2027 Preview was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Long story short, from my tests over the past 24 hours the Amazon Linux 2027 release is performing quite well and I didn't encounter any functional issues in testing this public preview while enjoying some nice incremental performance gains.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
